{
  "summary": "This study addresses the long-standing challenge of achieving stable photocatalytic pure water splitting over Ta3N5 by identifying incompatibility in cocatalysts between alkaline water oxidation systems and pure water splitting as a critical bottleneck. The researchers developed a highly dispersed RuCoOx cocatalyst using a multi-step incipient wetness impregnation strategy. The optimized Pt@Cr2O3/Ta3N5/RuCoOx photocatalyst achieved stoichiometric pure water splitting with an apparent quantum yield of 0.72% at 420 nm, the highest reported for Ta-based nitrides/oxynitrides. Mechanistic insights indicate enhanced interfacial electric fields and reduced reaction barriers improve hole extraction and surface kinetics.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Identification of cocatalyst incompatibility between alkaline water oxidation and pure water splitting as a critical bottleneck for Ta3N5 photocatalysts",
    "Development of a highly dispersed RuCoOx cocatalyst via multi-step incipient wetness impregnation",
    "Optimized Pt@Cr2O3/Ta3N5/RuCoOx achieves 0.72% apparent quantum yield for stoichiometric pure water splitting at 420 nm"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes cocatalyst compatibility as a fundamental design principle for stable photocatalytic water splitting over nitride-based materials, providing mechanistic insights for advancing solar-driven water splitting technologies."
}

{
  "summary": "This review reassesses the pro-tumor role of M2-like tumor-associated macrophages (TAMs) by evaluating functional evidence from tumor-derived cells. It examines phagocytosis, antigen processing, cross-presentation, and CD8+ T cell activation. Findings indicate that some CD206+ TAMs can cross-present tumor antigens and activate CD8+ T cells comparably to dendritic cells, correlating with improved outcomes. Conversely, other antigen-presenting macrophages may promote immunosuppression. The study emphasizes that antigen fate and co-stimulatory context, not M1/M2 markers, determine functional outcomes.",
  "key_findings": [
    "CD206+ tumor-associated macrophages can cross-present tumor antigens and activate CD8+ T cells at levels matching conventional dendritic cells",
    "These macrophages correlate with better clinical outcomes in specific contexts",
    "Antigen-presenting macrophages may retain cytotoxic T cells in tumor-promoting configurations or activate regulatory T cells"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This review establishes that functional capacity, not M1/M2 markers, dictates macrophage roles in tumor immunity, shifting focus from surface markers to antigen processing and co-stimulatory signals for accurate immunotherapeutic targeting."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates how early human settlement in the Arabian desert was influenced by both water availability and stone resources for toolmaking. Using a 1,500-km geoarchaeological survey across the Rub' al Khali, researchers found no lithic artifacts or knappable materials in areas with abundant freshwater but lacking stone. The analysis indicates that early humans required co-occurrence of water and lithic resources to settle, challenging models that prioritize water alone. This suggests raw-material geology significantly constrains dispersal patterns.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Palaeolake deposits were present at 55 of 125 surveyed localities but no lithic artifacts or knappable materials were found in areas with abundant freshwater.",
    "Spatial modeling shows 57% of water-accessible terrain lacks knappable stone, with raw-material availability improving site discrimination from an area under the curve of 0.695 to 0.768.",
    "Convergent patterns across Africa, North America, and Asia indicate that stone resource constraints, not water availability alone, limited early human dispersal."
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "The study demonstrates that lithic resource availability, not water, was a critical constraint on early human dispersal into arid regions, refining models of Pleistocene human migration."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigated how protein kinase A (PKA) signaling regulates group 3 innate lymphoid cell (ILC3) homeostasis and its role in gut inflammation. Using a single-cell atlas, researchers found that prostaglandin activates PKA to promote tissue-protective functions, while interleukin-23 (IL-23) suppresses PKA inhibitors. Deletion of PRKAR1A reduced ILC3s and increased infection susceptibility in mice. IL-23 overproduction decreased PRKAR1A, increasing PKA signaling and causing gut inflammation. Human ILC3s express PRKAR1A, which is downregulated by IL-23 or in inflammatory bowel disease.",
  "key_findings": [
    "ILC3s maintain tissue-protective functions via PKA signaling regulated by prostaglandin",
    "IL-23 suppresses PKA inhibitors, reducing ILC3 numbers and increasing infection susceptibility",
    "IL-23 overproduction decreases PRKAR1A, elevating PKA activity and triggering gut inflammation"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes PKA signaling as a critical regulator of ILC3 homeostasis, revealing how IL-23-driven PKA dysregulation contributes to intestinal inflammation."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigated how maternal and early life obesity affect adaptive immune responses during childhood vaccination. Using a mouse model replicating the obesity-epidemiological link, researchers found that high-fat diet exposure during pregnancy or early postnatal life, but not adulthood, altered naive CD4+ T cell epigenetics and transcriptional profiles. This impaired T follicular helper cell differentiation and germinal center reactions to mRNA and protein vaccines. Mechanistically, epigenetic repression of A20 (Tnfaip3) drove these defects, with pediatric A20 haploinsufficiency correlating with reduced TFH cells. These findings establish maternal and early life obesity as critical determinants of vaccine immunity during development.",
  "key_findings": [
    "High-fat diet exposure during pregnancy or early postnatal life, but not adulthood, reshaped naive CD4+ T cell epigenetics and transcriptional profiles",
    "Epigenetic repression of A20 (Tnfaip3) impaired T follicular helper cell differentiation and germinal center reactions to vaccines",
    "Pediatric A20 haploinsufficiency correlated with reduced circulating T follicular helper cells"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work identifies maternal and early life obesity as key determinants of adaptive immune responses during a critical developmental window, providing mechanistic insights into how obesity programs CD4+ T cell fate to impair vaccine efficacy."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates iron's melting temperature and electrical resistivity under Earth core pressures using microsecond pulsed Joule heating in diamond anvil cells. Over 300 reproducible melting measurements reveal a shallow melting curve at 6 K/GPa, reaching 4160 ± 80 K at 200 GPa. The findings suggest a cold core-mantle boundary (3430 K) and low adiabatic outer core heat flow (9.8 TW), indicating thermal convection could power the geodynamo before inner core formation.",
  "key_findings": [
    "A shallow melting curve for iron at 6 K/GPa, reaching 4160 ± 80 K at 200 GPa",
    "Inferred core-mantle boundary temperature of 3430 K",
    "Low adiabatic outer core heat flow of 9.8 TW"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "The study provides critical constraints on Earth's core thermal evolution, suggesting thermal convection alone could drive the geodynamo prior to inner core formation."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates how 4f electron filling influences bonding evolution in lanthanide single-atom catalysts across the series. Using structurally uniform catalysts, researchers identified a dual-periodic trend in binding energies, increasing from La to Eu and Gd to Yb. The half-filled 4f7 configuration at Eu-Gd transition drives abrupt changes, while consistent crystal-field effects enable robust linear relationships across coordination environments. These findings establish a 4f-electron-regulated framework for lanthanide reactivity.",
  "key_findings": [
    "A dual-periodic trend in lanthanide-support binding energies increases from La to Eu and Gd to Yb",
    "The half-filled 4f7 configuration induces an abrupt transition between Eu and Gd",
    "Consistent crystal-field effects enable linear relationships between binding and bonding energies across diverse coordination environments"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes a fundamental 4f-electron-regulated bonding framework for understanding lanthanide reactivity, providing intrinsic electronic principles for catalyst design across the lanthanide series."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigated regulatory T-cell dynamics in cirrhotic HCV genotype 4 patients during and after direct-acting antiviral therapy. Using longitudinal flow cytometry, researchers assessed T-cell subsets at baseline, week 4, treatment end, and 1 year post-SVR. Results showed baseline immune dysregulation with reduced T cells, exhausted CD8+ T cells, and expanded Tregs and TH17 cells. After successful therapy, Tregs normalized, exhausted CD8+ T cells decreased, and immune dysregulation partially improved, though further validation is needed.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Cirrhotic patients exhibited reduced CD3+ T cells and profound CD8+ T-cell exhaustion at baseline",
    "Tregs and TH17 cells were significantly elevated in cirrhotic patients compared to healthy controls",
    "Successful DAA therapy led to normalization of Tregs and reduction in exhausted CD8+ T cells by 1 year post-SVR"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This study demonstrates that successful HCV antiviral therapy in cirrhotic patients partially corrects peripheral immune dysregulation, providing insights into the immunological recovery post-viral clearance in chronic liver disease."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates whether brief dietary changes during acute inflammation influence long-term tissue immune memory. Using lean mice with type 17 skin inflammation, researchers found that a transient high-fat diet (HFD) exposure within a 4-day window at disease onset exacerbates inflammation to levels seen in chronic obesity. HFD outside this window or switching obese mice to low-fat diet during the window reduces disease severity. Mechanistically, HFD rapidly recruits neutrophils, which express Nlrp3 and Il1b, driving pathogenic TH17 cell activation. Post-recovery, a chromatin-poised tissue-resident memory T cell population persists, causing exaggerated inflammation upon recurrence despite subsequent low-fat diet. The findings demonstrate that short-lived dietary states during specific temporal windows can durably reprogram immune responses.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Transient high-fat diet exposure within a 4-day window at disease onset exacerbates acute type 17 skin inflammation to chronic obesity levels",
    "HFD outside this window or switching obese mice to low-fat diet during the window blunts disease amplification",
    "Neutrophil-mediated Nlrp3-Il1b axis licenses pathogenic TH17 cells, with persistent tissue-resident memory T cells causing exaggerated inflammation upon recurrence"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes that transient dietary states during narrow temporal windows can durably reprogram immune memory, revealing a mechanism by which short-lived physiological changes impact long-term inflammatory responses."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates how uniaxial strain affects electrical transport in magic-angle twisted bilayer graphene. By applying continuous strain near the magic angle (≈1.1°), researchers observed pronounced elastoresistance dependent on band filling and temperature. The gauge factor exceeded conventional metals by two orders of magnitude, with temperature-dependent behavior resembling Curie-. We discuss potential origins including nematic fluctuations and enhanced electronic entropy.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Elastoresistance in magic-angle twisted bilayer graphene exceeded conventional metals by two orders of magnitude",
    "Elastoresistance exhibited temperature dependence resembling Curie-Weiss behavior in specific doping regimes",
    "Potential origins include nematic fluctuations and enhanced electronic entropy from fluctuating isospin moments"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes uniaxial strain as a versatile probe for studying correlated electronic phenomena in moiré materials."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates dual sympathetic and parasympathetic nerve modulation to combat postoperative prostate cancer progression. Using personalized nanotechnology-enabled dual neurotherapy (ADNT), the researchers developed hybrid nanovesicles for codelivering β-blockers and muscarinic receptor antagonists. In murine models, ADNT delayed recurrence and metastasis, reduced autonomic nerve infiltration, and remodelled neuro-immune crosstalk and chemokine networks to promote antitumor immunity. It also alleviated cachexia and anxiety, achieving complete responses when combined with immune checkpoint blockade.",
  "key_findings": [
    "ADNT delays postoperative recurrence and metastasis in highly invasive murine prostate cancer models",
    "ADNT reduces autonomic nerve infiltration and remodels neuro-immune crosstalk and chemokine networks",
    "ADNT alleviates cancer-associated cachexia and anxiety, achieving complete responses with immune checkpoint blockade"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This approach demonstrates a novel dual neuromodulation strategy that effectively remodels the tumor microenvironment to enhance antitumor immunity and suppress progression in prostate cancer."
}

{
  "summary": "This study compared histopathological and transcriptomic features of immune checkpoint inhibitor-related T-cell-mediated rejection (irTCMR) versus conventional T-cell-mediated rejection (cTCMR) in liver transplant recipients. Using biopsy analysis and spatial transcriptomics, it found irTCMR exhibits higher steroid resistance (33.3% vs 0%), graft loss (41.7% vs 0%), and distinct transcriptomic profiles with 75 differentially expressed genes. These findings highlight irTCMR's aggressive phenotype and potential for targeted therapeutic strategies.",
  "key_findings": [
    "irTCMR demonstrates significantly higher steroid resistance (33.3% vs 0%) and graft loss (41.7% vs 0%) compared to cTCMR",
    "irTCMR shows increased rejection activity index with more severe bile duct inflammation and venous endothelialitis",
    "Spatial transcriptomics identified 75 differentially expressed genes in irTCMR, enriched for allograft rejection and antigen processing pathways"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This research establishes irTCMR as a distinct aggressive rejection phenotype with unique histopathological and transcriptomic characteristics, advancing understanding of immune checkpoint inhibitor-related rejection mechanisms in liver transplantation."
}

{
  "summary": "This study used genome-wide data from 4,587 ancient individuals across Bronze and Iron Age Europe to investigate Celtic language dispersal models. It identified three key demographic processes: Steppe-related ancestry arrival via Bell Beaker groups (4400 BP), Bronze Age migration from southwestern Europe to Britain, and westward expansion of Knovíz-Urnfield-related ancestry (3200-2800 BP). The analysis suggests eastern central European origins as the most parsimonious route for Celtic spread, though language shifts may not directly track genetic changes.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Steppe-related ancestry entered Europe via Bell Beaker-associated groups around 4400 BP",
    "Bronze Age migration from southwestern Europe to Britain occurred between 3800-3200 BP",
    "Knovíz-Urnfield-related ancestry expanded westward from eastern central Europe (3200-2800 BP) affecting France, the British Isles, northeastern Iberia, and Italy"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "The study provides genomic evidence supporting eastern central European origins as the most parsimonious demographic pathway for Celtic language dispersal in prehistoric Europe."
}

{
  "summary": "This study analyzed atmospheric oxygen and carbon dioxide data from 1990 to 2019 to quantify global carbon sinks. It found the ocean carbon sink strengthened by 0.63 ± 0.19 Pg C year-1 decade-1, accounting for 35% of fossil emissions. The net land sink showed no significant trend despite high variability, contributing 13% of emissions. These results contradict many process-based models, suggesting current models may underestimate future climate impacts.",
  "key_findings": [
    "The ocean carbon sink increased by 0.63 ± 0.19 Pg C year-1 decade-1 between 1990 and 2019",
    "The net land sink exhibited no robust trend, with a mean of 1.0 ± 0.5 Pg C year-1",
    "These findings challenge process-based models' accuracy in predicting future climate states"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "The study provides critical evidence that ocean carbon sinks have strengthened while land sinks remain stagnant, revealing a key gap in climate models that could impact future carbon cycle projections."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates direct growth of ultraclean monolayer amorphous carbon on sapphire substrates without metal catalysts. Using remote catalytic dehydrogenation in thermal chemical vapor deposition, the method achieves wafer-scale uniformity with no metallic residue. XPS confirms sp2-hybridized carbon networks, while Raman and AFM show atomically smooth monolayers exhibiting insulating behavior with gate-tunable transport. The approach enables device integration for applications like semiconductor membranes.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Wafer-scale monolayer amorphous carbon grown without metallic contamination",
    "XPS confirms sp2-hybridized carbon network with no catalyst residue",
    "Atomically smooth monolayers show insulating behavior with gate-tunable transport"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This method provides a contamination-free pathway for scalable production of ultraclean amorphous carbon films, enabling direct integration in electronic and optoelectronic devices without post-processing."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates how gamma-delta T cells overcome immunotherapy barriers in high-risk neuroblastoma by leveraging MHC-independent recognition mechanisms. The research examines γδ T cell platforms including non-engineered allogeneic products, composite γδ T-NK cell approaches, and CAR/AbTCR modifications. Key findings include γδ T cells being the primary antitumoral fraction in neuroblastoma lesions, their compatibility with the tumor immune microenvironment, and the potential of biomarker-driven patient selection to enhance efficacy.",
  "key_findings": [
    "γδ T cells constitute the primary antitumoral fraction in neuroblastoma lesions",
    "γδ T cells maintain efficacy regardless of MHC class I status through stress-induced ligand and phosphoantigen recognition",
    "Biomarker-driven patient selection improves γδ T cell therapy outcomes in neuroblastoma"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes γδ T cells as a promising immunotherapeutic strategy for neuroblastoma by bypassing MHC-dependent immune resistance mechanisms, offering a novel pathway to target tumors despite immunosuppressive microenvironments."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates domain-alteration chimeras (DALTACs) as a therapeutic strategy for castration-resistant prostate cancer. By engineering synthetic interactions between the androgen receptor (AR) ligand-binding domain and p300/CBP bromodomain, DALTAC-1 reprograms oncogenic complexes, suppressing AR-driven transcription and tumor growth more effectively than dual inhibition. It induces histone modifications and disrupts AR neo-enhancers, showing lineage-specific efficacy in preclinical models.",
  "key_findings": [
    "DALTAC-1 reprograms AR-p300/CBP interactions by enforcing non-native domain proximity, locking the complex in a non-productive state",
    "DALTAC-1 suppresses AR-driven transcription and proliferation more potently than combined AR and p300/CBP inhibition",
    "DALTAC-1 induces histone H2B N-terminal deacetylation and neomorphic AR acetylation, collapsing oncogenic AR neo-enhanceosomes"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This approach demonstrates a novel mechanism to disrupt oncogenic protein complexes through engineered domain-topology alterations, offering a targeted strategy for castration-resistant prostate cancer with potential broader applications in complex-driven diseases."
}

{
  "summary": "This study directly monitored fully submarine landslides at Homathko Delta, Canada, revealing slow upslope migration (breaching) rather than instantaneous failure. The findings indicate prolonged landslides generate sustained turbidity currents, transporting sediment, carbon, and pollutants offshore. This challenges previous assumptions about landslide dynamics and implications for seabed infrastructure design.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Direct underwater monitoring confirmed slow upslope migration (breaching) in fully submarine lands. ",
    "Prolonged landslides initiate sustained turbidity currents that transport sediment, carbon, and pollutants offshore. ",
    "The findings challenge assumptions about landslide failure mechanisms and stress requirements for seabed infrastructure."
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "The study provides direct evidence of breaching-type failure in natural submarine landslides, advancing understanding of their prolonged dynamics and implications for sediment transport and infrastructure resilience."
}

{
  "summary": "This study addresses the challenge of decoupling ionic conductivity from polymer segmental relaxation in solid polymer electrolytes (SPEs) by designing an ionic-ligand-assisted transport framework. Copolymerizing fluorinated acrylate with poly(ionic liquid) (PIL) and optimizing LiFSI salt concentration enhances Li+ conductivity to 0.38 mS cm-1 and transference number to 0.77 at 30°C. The electrolyte achieves 515 Wh kg-1 energy density with stable cycling in solid-state Li||NCM90 cells.",
  "key_findings": [
    "High ionic conductivity (0.38 mS cm-1) and transference number (0.77) at 30°C",
    "Fluorinated backbone and PIL synergistically expand oxidative stability to 4.94 V and form an inorganic-rich SEI",
    "Solid-state pouch cells achieve 515 Wh kg-1 energy density with stable cycling at 4.3 V for 600 cycles"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes an ionic-ligand-assisted transport mechanism for high-performance solid-state lithium batteries, enabling high energy density and stability without compromising interfacial integrity."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigated the prognostic role of regulatory T cells (Tregs) in follicular lymphoma (FL) patients treated with rituximab-lenalidomide. Using RNA-seq and multiplex immunofluorescence on clinical samples, it found Treg signatures and high infiltration correlated with shorter progression-free survival, particularly in the lenalidomide treatment arm. The results suggest Tregs may serve as a biomarker for risk stratification in FL.",
  "key_findings": [
    "RNA-seq identified Treg signatures associated with shorter progression-free survival in FL patients, especially those receiving rituximab-lenalidomide",
    "Multiplex immunofluorescence showed high CD3+/CD4+/FOXP3+ Treg infiltration correlated with inferior progression-free survival in lenalidomide-treated patients",
    "Extra-follicular Treg localization was specifically linked to shorter progression-free survival in lenalidomide-treated FL patients"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "The study provides evidence that regulatory T cell infiltration in follicular lymphoma may serve as an adverse prognostic biomarker for patients receiving rituximab-lenalidomide therapy."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates IgA's role in mucosal homeostasis by demonstrating that IgA deficiency in mice increases susceptibility to colorectal cancer. Using genetically controlled models, researchers found that IgA suppresses epithelial proliferation via DMBT1, an epithelial surface protein. IgA treatment reduced proliferation and tumor formation, with effects dependent on IgA's structural properties rather than specific binding. The findings reveal a mechanism by which IgA maintains mucosal health through microbiota-responsive epithelial regulation.",
  "key_findings": [
    "IgA deficiency in mice elevates colorectal cancer susceptibility",
    "IgA suppresses epithelial proliferation through DMBT1",
    "IgA's structural properties, not binding specificity, mediate tumor protection"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes IgA as a critical regulator of epithelial homeostasis by modulating microbiota-induced proliferation, providing a mechanistic basis for mucosal health maintenance."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates leveraging biological diversity and deep learning to construct a cell engineering toolkit. By integrating these approaches, the research aims to accelerate cellular engineering discoveries. The findings indicate a novel framework for efficient cell manipulation, potentially enhancing precision in biological applications without specifying exact mechanisms or outcomes.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Integration of biological diversity with deep learning for cell engineering",
    "Development of a framework enabling efficient cellular manipulation",
    "Potential enhancement of precision in biological applications"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "The study advances the field by demonstrating a synergistic approach combining biological complexity and machine learning to create adaptable cell engineering solutions."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigated a biointerface-customized nanomodulator designed to induce mitochondria-specific dysfunction for tumor immunity reprogramming. The approach involved engineering nanomodulators with sequential targeting and controlled activation to achieve preferential tumor accumulation and mitochondria-directed localization. Co-delivered calcium donors and efflux inhibitors, amplified by photothermal activation, induced mitochondrial dysfunction, promoting immunogenic cell death and antitumor immune responses in mice.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Nanomodulators achieved mitochondria-specific dysfunction via biointer:engineered targeting and photothermal activation",
    "Co-delivered calcium donors and efflux inhibitors induced mitochondrial ion interference",
    "The strategy promoted immunogenic cell death and tumor regression in murine models"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work demonstrates a subcellular precision strategy for inducing immune-beneficial mitochondrial dysfunction without systemic toxicity, advancing targeted immunomodulation in cancer therapy."
}

{
  "summary": "This review examines short-chain fatty acids (SCFAs) as modulators of the tumor microenvironment (TME). SCFAs influence metabolic pathways, immune responses, and epigenetic mechanisms in cancer cells, with effects varying by SCFA type, concentration, tumor context, and microenvironment. While preclinical studies suggest therapeutic potential, clinical efficacy remains limited due to poor bioavailability and rapid metabolism. The study highlights context-dependent outcomes and unresolved challenges for clinical translation.",
  "key_findings": [
    "SCFAs modulate tumor metabolism through glycolysis, lipid biosynthesis, and oxidative phosphorylation",
    "SCFAs affect immune cell function by enhancing cytotoxic T lymphocytes and NK cells while promoting regulatory T cells and myeloid-derived suppressor cells",
    "SCFAs epigenetically regulate gene expression via histone deacetylase inhibition and microRNA/lncRNA modulation"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This review clarifies the context-dependent dual roles of SCFAs in tumor microenvironments, providing a foundation for targeted therapeutic strategies despite current bioavailability and clinical translation challenges."
}
